{const a=Math.acos(.8),top=sample(t=>Math.sqrt(Math.max(0,4-20*(1-Math.cos(t)))),-a,a,64);P('pendulum-energy-contour','One motion follows one energy loop','This loop has energy 2 J. At angle zero it crosses momentum +2 and −2 kg·m²/s. At the left and right ends the momentum is zero and the swing turns back. This is phase space, not the pendulum’s path in the room.','Angle from bottom (rad)',[-.8,.8],'Angular momentum (kg·m²/s)',[-2.5,2.5],[S('Energy 2 J',top.concat([...top].reverse().map(([t,p])=>[t,-p]))) ]);}
P('pendulum-energy-barrier','Thirty joules cannot reach the top','The potential reaches 40 J at the upright angles ±π. The 30 J total-energy line meets it earlier, so the pendulum turns back.','Angle from bottom (rad)',[-Math.PI,Math.PI],'Energy (J)',[0,40],[S('Potential energy',sample(a=>20*(1-Math.cos(a)),-Math.PI,Math.PI,64)),S('Total energy 30 J',[[-Math.PI,30],[Math.PI,30]])]);
P('pendulum-separatrix','The boundary reaches 4 rad/s at the bottom','For the stated pendulum, these two branches separate swinging from full rotation. At ±π the boundary speed is zero. This is a graph of angle and rate, not a path through space.','Angle from bottom (rad)',[-Math.PI,Math.PI],'Angular rate (rad/s)',[-4,4],[S('Positive boundary rate',sample(a=>4*Math.cos(a/2),-Math.PI,Math.PI,64)),S('Negative boundary rate',sample(a=>-4*Math.cos(a/2),-Math.PI,Math.PI,64))]);
P('sensitive-dependence','A small starting gap grows','The local estimate doubles from 0.001 to 0.002 and then 0.004. This short-interval model is not a guarantee of unlimited growth.','Time (s)',[0,2],'Separation (example units)',[0,.004],[S('Local growth estimate',sample(t=>.001*2**t,0,2))]);
review('fluid-dynamics');
P('parcel-path','Follow one parcel','This parcel starts at 1 m and moves right at 2 m/s. After 3 s it is at 7 m.','Time (s)',[0,3],'Parcel position (m)',[0,8],[S('Parcel position',[[0,1],[3,7]])]);
P('couette-profile','The fluid speed rises across the gap','Fluid at the fixed plate has zero speed. Halfway across, at 0.01 m, speed is 0.3 m/s; at the moving plate it is 0.6 m/s.','Distance from fixed plate (m)',[0,.02],'Fluid speed along plates (m/s)',[0,.6],[S('Steady velocity profile',[[0,0],[.01,.3],[.02,.6]])]);
P('pipe-profile','The centre is fastest','At the centre, speed is 1 m/s. At radius 0.5 m it is 0.75 m/s; at the wall, radius 1 m, it is zero. This plots speed at each radius, not a parcel’s path.','Radius from pipe centre (m)',[0,1],'Flow speed (m/s)',[0,1],[S('Laminar pipe profile',sample(r=>1-r*r,0,1))]);
P('poiseuille-flow','Radius has a strong effect on flow','Doubling radius gives sixteen times the flow: 0.016 m³/s. Pressure drop, viscosity and length stay fixed, and the flow must remain laminar.','Radius / original radius',[1,2],'Volume flow (m³/s)',[0,.016],[S('Laminar flow rule',sample(r=>.001*r**4,1,2))]);
P('boundary-layer-scale','The wall-affected layer grows downstream','At 1 m downstream, the thickness scale is 0.01 m. This is a scale estimate, not an exact boundary or a full velocity profile.','Distance along wall (m)',[0,4],'Thickness scale (m)',[0,.02],[S('Layer thickness scale',sample(x=>.01*Math.sqrt(x),0,4,64))]);
B('fluctuation-size','Opposite fluctuations still have size','The two readings cancel in the mean, but each is 2 m/s away from it. Squaring before averaging gives an RMS size of 2 m/s.','Velocity fluctuation (m/s)',[['First',2],['Second',-2]]);
review('plasma');
P('shielded-potential','Shielding reduces the same-distance potential','At two Debye lengths, the potential is about 0.135 of the unscreened value at that radius. The graph shows this ratio, not the full potential’s additional 1/r decrease.','Radius / Debye length',[0,4],'Screened / unscreened potential',[0,1],[S('Shielding factor',sample(r=>Math.exp(-r),0,4))]);
P('gyroradius','A perpendicular orbit has radius 3 m','With no other forces and uniform field, speed 6 m/s and turn rate 2 rad/s give radius 3 m. The circle shows the perpendicular plane; motion along the field is not included.','First perpendicular direction (m)',[-4,4],'Second perpendicular direction (m)',[-4,4],[S('Orbit',Array.from({length:65},(_,i)=>[3*Math.cos(i*Math.PI/32),3*Math.sin(i*Math.PI/32)]))]);
P('magnetic-moment','Perpendicular energy follows field strength','As long as the magnetic moment stays fixed, going from 1 T to 2 T raises this energy from 3 eV to 6 eV.','Magnetic field strength (T)',[1,2],'Perpendicular kinetic energy (eV)',[0,6],[S('Constant-moment relation',[[1,3],[2,6]])]);
P('magnetic-pressure','Twice the field gives four times the pressure','The original field has pressure scale 5 Pa. Doubling that field gives 20 Pa because the rule squares the field strength.','Field / original field',[0,2],'Magnetic pressure (Pa)',[0,20],[S('Magnetic pressure scale',sample(b=>5*b*b,0,2))]);
P('electron-restoring-motion','Displacement and acceleration have opposite signs','At displacement +0.02 m the acceleration is −0.18 m/s². This is a force-rule graph; it does not assume a starting velocity.','Electron displacement (m)',[-.02,.02],'Acceleration (m/s²)',[-.18,.18],[S('Restoring rule',[[-.02,.18],[.02,-.18]])]);
P('linear-growth','Positive growth rate makes the amplitude rise','The model doubles each second: 0.01, 0.02, then 0.04. A constant growth rate is assumed only over the interval where this small-amplitude model applies.','Time (s)',[0,2],'Amplitude (example units)',[0,.04],[S('Growing mode',sample(t=>.01*2**t,0,2))]);
B('velocity-distribution','Add the particles in the separate groups','The two non-overlapping groups contribute 3 and 5 particles/m³. Their sum is 8 particles/m³. The example does not specify their velocity widths, so these are counts by group, not a density-versus-velocity curve.','Number density (particles/m³)',[['First group',3],['Second group',5]]);
review('general-relativity');
R('metric-distance','One gap, two matching scales','Here g = 4, so each coordinate metre represents 2 measured metres. A coordinate change of 3 m gives 6 m of measured length. This scale change alone does not prove that space is curved.',[['Coordinate change (m)',[0,1,2,3]],['Measured length (m)',[0,2,4,6]]]);
R('clock-time','Coordinate time and this clock’s time','The fixed factor 0.8 gives 8 clock seconds for 10 coordinate seconds. These aligned marks describe the same interval.',[['Coordinate time (s)',[0,2.5,5,7.5,10]],['Clock time (s)',[0,2,4,6,8]]]);
P('tidal-curvature','Relative acceleration points against separation','For the stated curvature component, a separation of +0.2 m gives relative acceleration −0.1 m/s². The sign describes motion toward smaller separation.','Separation (m)',[-.2,.2],'Relative acceleration (m/s²)',[-.1,.1],[S('Local tidal rule',[[-.2,.1],[.2,-.1]])]);
P('static-clock','A supported clock ticks more slowly near the horizon','At horizon-radius / clock-radius = 0.36, the clock factor is 0.8. This curve is for a held-still clock outside the horizon, not a freely falling clock.','Horizon radius / clock radius',[0,.96],'Clock time / faraway time',[0,1],[S('Static clock factor',sample(u=>Math.sqrt(1-u),0,.96,48))]);
P('weak-light-bending','A larger passing distance gives a smaller bend','The weak-field bend is 0.004 rad at the first passing distance and 0.002 rad at twice that distance. Mass stays fixed.','Passing distance / first passing distance',[1,2],'Bending angle (rad)',[0,.004],[S('Weak-field bending',sample(b=>.004/b,1,2))]);
R('cosmic-scale','The same comoving gap at scale factor 2','Each comoving Mpc corresponds to 2 physical Mpc at this chosen time. The marked gap of 3 comoving Mpc is 6 physical Mpc.',[['Comoving separation (Mpc)',[0,1,2,3]],['Physical separation (Mpc)',[0,2,4,6]]]);
P('friedmann-rate','Four times the density doubles the rate','For this expanding, flat model with no cosmological constant, the rate grows with the square root of density.','Density / original density',[1,4],'Expansion rate (chosen inverse-time unit)',[0,20],[S('Expansion rate',sample(r=>10*Math.sqrt(r),1,4))]);
review('experimental-methods');
C('offset-correction','Separate the reading from the extra amount','Each square stands for 1 g. All 52 squares make up the displayed reading. The 2 pale squares are the known extra amount. The 50 dark squares give the corrected estimate. This picture does not show the remaining uncertainty.',52,50,13);
P('repeat-mean','Readings sit around their mean','The three dots are 9, 10 and 11 mm. The dashed mean line is 10 mm. Reading number shows order, not elapsed time.','Reading number',[1,3],'Measured length (mm)',[8,12],[S('Readings',[[1,9],[2,10],[3,11]],{join:false}),S('Mean',[[1,10],[3,10]])]);
P('mean-uncertainty','Independent repeats reduce uncertainty in the mean','With scatter fixed at 8 mm, 16 independent readings give a mean uncertainty of 2 mm. This rule does not remove a shared bias.','Independent reading count',[1,16],'Mean standard uncertainty (mm)',[0,8],[S('Uncertainty at whole counts',Array.from({length:16},(_,i)=>[i+1,8/Math.sqrt(i+1)]),{join:false})]);
P('calibration-gain','Read the sensor rule backward','The line links true input and displayed output. Output 7 V corresponds to input 3 V after correcting both gain and offset.','Reference input (V)',[0,4],'Sensor output (V)',[0,9],[S('Calibration line',[[0,1],[4,9]]),S('Example reading',[[3,7]],{join:false})]);
B('calibration-hysteresis','The same force gives different readings','The bars are readings at the same reference force after different approaches. Their difference is 0.4 N; the example does not provide a full hysteresis curve.','Gauge reading (N)',[['From above',10.2],['From below',9.8]]);
P('sample-scatter','Measure each reading’s distance from the mean','The mean is 4 s. The readings differ from it by −2, 0 and +2 s; the sample standard deviation is 2 s.','Reading number',[1,3],'Measured interval (s)',[0,8],[S('Readings',[[1,2],[2,4],[3,6]],{join:false}),S('Mean',[[1,4],[3,4]])]);
B('least-squares','Both signs count toward the fitting error','These are the three stated residuals: observed minus predicted values. Squaring gives 1, 0 and 4 V², whose sum is 5 V².','Residual (V)',[['Reading 1',-1],['Reading 2',0],['Reading 3',2]]);
B('weighted-fit','The more precise reading gets more weight','Doubling standard uncertainty from 1 V to 2 V reduces the inverse-variance weight to one quarter.','Weight (1/V²)',[['Uncertainty 1 V',1],['Uncertainty 2 V',.25]]);
R('report-resolution','The reported result and uncertainty use matching digits','The centre is 12.35 V. The endpoints are the reported value minus and plus 0.13 V. The coverage factor and measurement conditions must accompany this interval.',[['Reported voltage (V)',[12.22,12.35,12.48]]]);
